course: medicine
lesson: 65
title: VAR rows, referees, fans and rivalries
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When your team is losing, adrenaline makes you certain you're right, but a fan 60 m up in the stand sees from one angle while the ref is often 10 m away, and the replay quite often shows the ref got it right.
explain: Darnell screams «VAR, check EVERYTHING!» at the telly, but VAR only looks at four things, goals, penalties, straight red cards and mistaken identity, and only steps in for clear and obvious errors, so a throw-in stays with the ref.
explain: Referees are athletes too, running about 10 to 12 km a match, and in kids' football many refs are teenagers; shouting and abuse are a big reason they quit, and with no refs there are no matches.
explain: Rivalry is fun as banter, and Glasgow brothers can say «I'm no talkin' tae you till Monday» (Scots for I'm not talking to you) and still eat dinner together at six; it turns bad when it becomes hate, abuse online or a dangerous crowd.
fact: VAR checks only four kinds of decision: goals, penalties, straight red cards and mistaken identity, and only for clear and obvious errors.
fact: VAR was used at the 2018 World Cup and came to the Premier League a year later, in 2019.
fact: At the 2022 World Cup, semi-automated offside used cameras and a sensor in the ball that sent data 500 times a second; offside is judged on body parts you can score with, not the arms.
fact: Top referees run about 10 to 12 km in a match, nearly as far as many players, and have to pass fitness tests.
fact: Many grassroots referees are teenagers, and abuse from the touchline is a big reason referees give up.
fact: Anger and adrenaline make people feel sure they are right, and fans far from the play see from one angle.
fact: When matches were played without crowds in 2020, studies reported that home advantage shrank, but that does not make booing fair, and booing your own players knocks their confidence.
fact: Derbies are games between local rivals; rivalry is fine as banter, not as fights or hate.
fact: At the Hillsborough disaster in 1989, 97 Liverpool fans died in a crush; inquests in 2016 found they were unlawfully killed and that the fans were not to blame, and stadiums today have stewards and seats.
fact: At a big match, children stay with their adult, agree a meeting point, and tell a steward if the crowd feels too tight.
fact: After England lost the Euro 2020 final on penalties, three players who missed were racially abused online, and some of the abusers were prosecuted; the right move with a hateful post is to report it, not share it.
fact: Under the Laws of the Game, arguing with the referee can bring a yellow card, abusive language a red card, and the team then plays with one fewer.
fact: «Pen» is short for penalty, «all day» means definitely, and «kidda» is a Liverpool word for kid.
exercise: Darnell: «VAR, check EVERYTHING! That was a throw-in!» Does VAR check throw-ins? | No, only goals, penalties, straight reds and identity | Yes, every decision in the match | Yes, but only in the second half | No, VAR checks only offsides
exercise: How many kinds of decision does VAR check? | 4 | 1 | 10 | Every one
exercise: When did VAR come to the Premier League? | 2019 | 2018 | 2010 | 2022
exercise: Fin: «Offside by a toenail? That's made up, so it is». How was offside measured in 2022? | Cameras and a ball sensor | The crowd's loudest shout | A guess by the linesman | A coin toss
exercise: Which body part doesn't count for offside? | The arms | The feet | The head | The knees
exercise: Mia: «The ref just stands about, he's not even fit». How far do top refs run? | About 10 to 12 km | About 1 km, mostly walking | About 50 m from the centre circle | About 40 km, like a marathon
exercise: The dad bellows at the 14-year-old ref. What's a big reason young refs quit? | Abuse from the touchline | Too much running in every match | Boring black kits they have to wear | Not enough whistles to go round
exercise: Nathan, high in the stand: «That's a pen all day!» Replay: no contact. Why did he see it wrong? | He was far away at one angle | Fans always see better than the ref | The ref was asleep on his feet | Replays are fake and can't be trusted
exercise: Fan 60 m away, ref 10 m away. How much further is the fan? | 50 m | 70 m | 6 m | 10 m
exercise: Sophie: «Booing the ref works». What did empty-stadium studies report? | Home advantage shrank without crowds | Crowds never affect anything | Booing makes refs braver and give fewer fouls | Empty stadiums had more goals
exercise: The Glasgow brothers support Celtic and Rangers. What's a derby? | A game between local rivals | A game played with no referee at all | A friendly where nobody keeps the score | A match played on a horse racecourse
exercise: Jamie's first big match: «Can I go off on my own at half-time?» Dad's plan? | Stay together and tell a steward if it's too tight | Go alone, he knows the way | Meet back after full time | Push to the front if it's busy
exercise: What did the 2016 Hillsborough inquests find? | The 97 fans were unlawfully killed and not to blame | The fans caused the crush | Nobody died in the crush | It was a match with no fans
exercise: Rohan: «It's just online, it's banter, innit?» What happened after the Euro 2020 final? | Players were racially abused and some abusers prosecuted | Nothing, online words don't count | The players were banned | The final was replayed
exercise: Charlie swears at the ref in his own match. What do the Laws say? | Abusive language is a red card | A warning only, and then play on as normal | A free kick for his team | Nothing, if the ref was wrong
exercise: After Charlie's red, how many players does his team have left? | 10 | 11 | 9 | 12
test: Ayesha whispers «That's well out of order». What would happen if every ref got shouted at like that? | Many would quit, and there'd be no matches | Refs would get much better and make fewer mistakes | Matches would be fairer | Nothing would change
test: Darnell's mate: «VAR re-referees the whole game». What's true? | It's limited to 4 kinds of decision | It replays every throw-in and every corner | It picks the man of the match | It decides the half-time score
test: Fin's dad: «Tight offsides are just guesses». How did 2022 measure them? | A ball sensor sending data 500 times a second | Linesmen guessing as best they can from the halfway line | The players voting | Counting the fans who shouted
test: Mia, after reading that refs pass fitness tests: which is more accurate? | Refs run nearly as far as many players | Refs walk about 1 km | Refs never need to run because they stand in the middle | Refs ride bikes on the pitch
test: Sophie's team boos its own striker after a miss. What usually happens? | His confidence drops | He scores a hat-trick | The ref gives a penalty | Nothing at all
test: Nathan sees the replay with no contact. What's the lesson? | Wait for the replay before blowing your top | Fans are always right, whatever the replay shows | Replays always lie, so trust your own eyes instead | Shout even louder next time so the ref hears you
test: Mum to the Glasgow brothers: «Aye, ye will. Dinner's at six». What does she mean? | They'll talk to each other at dinner anyway | They'll eat separately in their own rooms till Monday | Dinner is cancelled on derby day | They must support the same team
test: Jamie's dad says «Stay with me, kidda». What does «kidda» mean? | Kid | Goalkeeper | Steward | Ticket
test: A cartoon fan says «Real fans hate the other team». What's true? | Real fans love their team and can respect the other | Real fans must hate the rivals | Respect is against football rules | Fans can't enjoy a derby
test: Rohan wants to share a mean post «as a joke». Best move? | Report it, don't share it | Share it to make it go away | Add his own comment to it | Post it on the team page
test: A dad yells abuse at a teenage ref and then says «I'm right though». Does being right make abuse OK? | No, abuse is wrong right or wrong | Yes, as long as the ref really did get it wrong | Yes, if he's a parent | No, but only at under-10s
exam: Identify the categories of decision subject to review by the video assistant referee. | Goals, penalties, straight red cards and mistaken identity | All decisions without exception | Throw-ins and corner kicks only | The selection of the starting players
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the physical demands placed upon a top referee? | The referee covers approximately ten to twelve kilometres in a match | The referee remains largely stationary | The referee covers approximately one kilometre | The referee is exempt from fitness testing
exam: It is given that a player employs abusive language towards the referee. State the sanction prescribed under the Laws of the Game. | Dismissal by means of a red card | No sanction of any description | A free kick awarded to the player's team | A verbal reminder only
exam: Ascertain the conclusion reached by the inquests of 2016 in respect of the Hillsborough disaster. | The victims were unlawfully killed and the supporters bore no blame | The supporters were responsible for the disaster | No fatalities occurred | The inquiry reached no conclusion whatsoever
